course: dictionary
lesson: 12516
title: Words that put people down
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Derogatory means showing a low opinion of someone, and meant to insult or put them down. Look for clues: the words make someone sound small, foolish or less worthy than others.
explain: «Her derogatory comment about his shoes made the whole class laugh at him.» The word comment tells you someone said something. Putting someone down means making them feel small, so a derogatory comment is an unkind remark that does this. Check: "an unkind remark meant to make him feel small" fits the sentence, so derogatory is right.
explain: Lessons on respect teach that derogatory words about someone's race, religion, disability, looks or gender are hurtful and not allowed. Dictionaries mark insulting words with «(derogatory)», so when you see that mark, be careful with that word.
fact: Derogatory is an adjective, so it describes a word, remark or comment.
fact: «Derogatory» means showing a low opinion of someone, and meant to insult or put them down.
fact: A derogatory term is a word used to insult a person or a group of people.
fact: In dictionaries, «(derogatory)» marks an insulting word.
fact: Derogatory remarks about race, religion, disability, looks or gender are hurtful and not allowed.
exercise: «Her derogatory comment about his shoes made the whole class laugh at him.» What does derogatory mean? | Very loud and noisy | *Showing a low opinion and meant to insult | Long and boring | Kind and full of praise
exercise: «The coach made derogatory remarks about our team, so we all felt small.» Which word means the same as derogatory here? | *Insulting | Cheerful | Friendly | Helpful
exercise: Mia says a derogatory term is just a funny nickname, so it does not matter. What is the right answer? | A derogatory term is always a compliment | Derogatory means very old | It is just a funny nickname, so it is always fine | *A derogatory term is meant to put someone down, so it is hurtful and not allowed
test: «She ignored the derogatory name-calling and walked on with her head high.» What does derogatory describe? | The sky | The walk | Her head | *The name-calling, which is meant to insult
test: Which sentence shows a derogatory opinion of someone? | *«Nobody wants you here, you are useless.» | «The park is open until six.» | «Thank you for helping me with my bag.» | «Your drawing is lovely, well done.»
exam: Why is the word (derogatory) marked in a dictionary? | To show it is a fancy word | To show it is a noun | To show it is a new word | *To show it is a rude, insulting word
